# Copy to .env and fill in. Only WXT_-prefixed variables reach the extension bundle; anything else
# here stays on your machine and is used by the tools in tools/ and by the Supabase CLI.
#
# The two WXT_ values are safe to ship in a built extension and are meant to be public: the
# publishable key authorises nothing on its own, because every row-level security policy is written
# `to authenticated` and the `anon` role holds no grant on any table. `pnpm check:rls` asserts that.

# Your Supabase project's API URL, e.g. https://abcdefghijkl.supabase.co
WXT_SUPABASE_URL=

# The project's publishable (anon) key.
WXT_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# The same two values again, under the names the website's bundler insists on. Next.js only inlines
# variables prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ and WXT only exposes ones prefixed WXT_, so one pair of values
# needs two pairs of names. Copy them across verbatim — a website pointed at a different Supabase
# project from the extension is two house hunts that cannot see each other. On Vercel these two are
# set in the project's own environment and this file is not present at all.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Where the website lives. The extension opens it when the icon is clicked, and injects its bridge
# content script on this origin and no other — so this is both a destination and a trust boundary
# (design D3, D5). Origin only, no trailing slash. Development: http://localhost:3100
WXT_WEB_APP_URL=

# --- below here: never shipped, used only by tools and deploys ---

# The project ref alone (the subdomain from the URL above). Used by `pnpm deploy:function`.
SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=

# Every origin the website is served on, comma-separated, e.g.
# https://www.househunt.london,https://house-hunt-web-sigma.vercel.app (origins only, no trailing
# slash, no spaces needed). This is NOT bundled — it is set as a *secret on the Edge Functions* so
# their CORS layer (supabase/functions/_shared/http.ts) allows the website to read replies; an origin
# missing from this list makes the functions answer `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: null` to it — the
# extension and Rightmove origins are matched separately and still work — so every call *from the
# website* is blocked by the browser.
#
# (`WEB_APP_ORIGIN` was here. It listed the origins the Edge Functions would accept as the website,
# and there are no Edge Functions. A route on the site is same-origin to a page, and same-origin
# requests are never preflighted, so the site's own names need no allow-list at all; what does need
# one is the extension and a Rightmove content script, and `apps/web/src/server/cors.ts` decides
# those by scheme and by an exact host rather than from configuration that can go stale.)

# The secret key (`sb_secret_…`), for the API routes under `apps/web/src/app/api/`. The counterpart
# of the publishable key above, under Supabase's current API keys. Only needed to run those routes
# locally (`pnpm dev:web`); in production it is a variable on the Vercel project.
#
# The routes also accept the legacy `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`, because that is the name the
# Supabase↔Vercel integration syncs — a project with that integration connected already has a
# working key under a name nobody chose. Prefer this one when setting it by hand.
#
# Edge Functions never needed either in a file, since the platform injected one, which is why this
# is new here and why `SETUP.md` used to say not to set it. A route on Vercel is injected nothing.
#
# It bypasses every row-level security policy. It belongs in this file and in the Vercel project,
# and nowhere else: never in a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` name, never in the extension bundle.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# The analysis route's OpenAI key. On Vercel it is a project environment variable; here it is what
# `pnpm dev:web` reads. It used to be a Supabase project secret, set with `supabase secrets set` —
# doing that now sets a secret nothing reads while every analysis fails saying the key is missing.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# What one analysis reserves against both spend caps before it is made, in dollars. Optional; the
# route defaults to 0.10. Worth setting only if a listing's real cost has drifted far enough from
# that for the reservation to stop bounding anything.
ANALYSIS_ESTIMATE_USD=

# TfL's journey planner, from https://api-portal.tfl.gov.uk, read by `apps/web/src/app/api/travel`.
# On Vercel these are project environment variables; here they are what `pnpm dev:web` reads.
#
# Optional, and it degrades rather than breaks — but the allowance is per minute and the tiers are
# far apart: 50 requests a minute unkeyed, 500 keyed. The backfill schedule is sized to sit inside
# the keyed allowance alongside people browsing.
#
# A subscription comes with two keys so one can be rotated while the other keeps serving. They are
# two credentials against one quota, so alternating them buys nothing: the primary is what is sent
# and the secondary is the standby. `TFL_APP_KEY` is read last, for a deployment set up before the
# pair existed.
TFL_PRIMARY_KEY=
TFL_SECONDARY_KEY=

# What the pg_cron backfill schedule sends in `X-Backfill-Token` to prove it is the schedule rather
# than a person. It must be the same string the vault holds as `travel_backfill_token` — SETUP.md
# sets both from one shell so they cannot drift — and on Vercel it is a project environment
# variable.
#
# An unset token is not a wildcard: `travel` answers "not the schedule" to every caller and falls
# through to the ordinary person's checks, so leaving this empty locally is right. No harness runs a
# backfill.
TRAVEL_BACKFILL_TOKEN=

# The database password, for connecting with psql to apply migrations by hand.
SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD=
